# RELEASE 15 BUG (a real one, in the 1981 game). "drop all" here
# drives the room-contents lister into infinite recursion: the
# routine at packed address 6626 calls itself forever and never
# advances to the next object. On period interpreters those nested
# frames blew the fixed stack and the game crashed; on an interpreter
# with an unbounded stack it hangs. Probed empirically: the call
# fires tens of thousands of times, the instruction after it never
# runs, and the call depth grows without limit -- while the object
# tree stays perfectly consistent, so this is the game's logic, not
# data corruption. Note the game's own platform contract caps total
# stack usage (Z-Machine Standard 6.3.3); release 15 breaks it.
#
# The commands below are fenced off until a route around the bug is
# found. Triggering it: reach this room and type "drop all" (or any
# inventory walk, such as "i").

```
drop all
```

